\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Occupation signs split the half-spin carrier}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.spinAction_diagonalBivector_basis,TauCeti.iSup_spinWeightSpace_even_eq_spinPlus,TauCeti.iSup_spinWeightSpace_odd_eq_spinMinus}

\p{Let #{V=W\oplus W'} be a complex nondegenerate quadratic space of
dimension #{2\ell}, with #{\ell\geq 1}. The summands are maximal
isotropic and dual under the polar form #{B_Q}. Choose dual bases
#{w_i\in W} and #{w_i'\in W'} with #{B_Q(w_i,w_j')=\delta_{ij}}.
On #{S=\bigwedge W}, write #{u_I} for the wedge basis vector indexed
by #{I}. The even and odd exterior degrees are #{S^+} and #{S^-}.}

\p{The diagonal Clifford bivector of \ref{fcap-000N}

##{\widehat H_i=\beta_Q(w_i,w_i')
=\tfrac12[\iota_Q(w_i),\iota_Q(w_i')]}

has factors acting by #{c_i=\varepsilon_{w_i}} and #{a_i=a_{w_i'}} in the
notation of \ref{fcap-0027}. Write #{H_i} for the resulting endomorphism of
#{S}. The relation #{c_i a_i+a_i c_i=1} gives

##{N_i:=c_i a_i,\qquad
H_i=N_i-\tfrac12\operatorname{id}_S.}

Since #{N_i} projects onto the vectors containing #{i},

##{H_i u_I=\begin{cases}
\frac12 u_I,&i\in I,\\
-\frac12 u_I,&i\notin I.
\end{cases}}

The simultaneous weight space for each sign pattern is the line
#{\mathbb C u_I}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.spinWeightSpace_spinWeight}. The lines
with even #{|I|} sum to #{S^+}, and those with odd #{|I|} sum to
#{S^-}. Each half has dimension #{2^{\ell-1}}, while #{S} has
dimension #{2^\ell}.}

\p{For #{\ell=2}, the occupation test gives the complete table

##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
I&u_I&(H_1,H_2)&\text{summand}\\ \hline
\varnothing&1&(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac12)&S^+\\
\{1\}&w_1&(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12)&S^-\\
\{2\}&w_2&(-\tfrac12,\tfrac12)&S^-\\
\{1,2\}&w_1\wedge w_2&(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)&S^+
\end{array}}

Every entry is obtained by applying the two rank-one operators of
\ref{fcap-001P}; the four displayed lines exhaust #{S} in this case.}

\p{Fulton--Harris use #{B_Q=2Q_{\mathrm{FH}}}, so their standard hyperbolic
basis becomes polar-dual here after setting #{w_i=e_i} and
#{w_i'=\frac12e_{\ell+i}}. Their represented operator is then
#{L_{e_i}D_{e_i^*}-\frac12 I=N_i-\frac12 I}, assigning the same positive
sign to an occupied coordinate
\citet{Proposition 20.15, proof, pp. 305--306}{fulton2004representation}.
Thus their occupation signs and even/odd exterior-degree labels agree with the
displayed TauCeti axes.}

\p{Goodman--Wallach realize the complex spinor carrier as the exterior
algebra of the dual isotropic space
\citet{Theorem 6.1.3, proof, pp. 304--305}{goodman2009symmetry}.
Identify their creation space #{W_{\mathrm{GW}}^*} with #{W} here,
and their annihilation space #{W_{\mathrm{GW}}} with #{W'}, taking
#{w_i=e_{-i}} and #{w_i'=e_i}.}

\p{The reversed order in
\citet{Lemma 6.2.2 and (6.14), p. 313}{goodman2009symmetry} gives

##{\widehat H_i^{\mathrm{Tau}}=\beta_Q(e_{-i},e_i)
=-\widehat H_i^{\mathrm{GW}}.}

Accordingly, their occupied eigenvalue #{-\frac12} and unoccupied eigenvalue
#{+\frac12} negate every pair in the table
\citet{Proposition 6.2.3, proof, pp. 314--315}{goodman2009symmetry}.
The exterior vectors and parity labels remain fixed: #{S^+} means even
#{|I|}, and #{S^-} means odd #{|I|}. This records the occupation operators,
weight lines, and parity decomposition, without a root-system,
highest-weight, or irreducibility assertion.}
